{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537539115,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NjAyOTA3MzQ="},"added":["severity-normal"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537556591,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzY0MDM1Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2858#issuecomment-423640356"},"message":"As shown in #2859, the key is that the subdataset must not also be a subdataset of the parent's origin to let the faulty behavior occur.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537556942,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzY0MTg0MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2858#issuecomment-423641841"},"message":"Most likely we are looking at an order effect. The parent ds is updated first, and if it doesn't get the new subdataset state from its update, it will never get saved.\n\nI guess it just needs a save run at the end.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537599299,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NjE0ODk0OTk="},"added":["fix-implemented"],"removed":[]},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537765308,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTg2MjMyODc1NQ=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1537788046,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzk0MjY4OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2862#issuecomment-423942688"},"message":"The different behavior of `save` and `add` as @yarikoptic pointed out, is something to clean up - yes. But  how to do it does indeed depend on figuring out a logic, that is valid for v6. That's currently not the case. \n\nI don't see how we could or why we should possibly avoid having those pointer files. First, it's standard behavior of `git-annex` for an unlocked file with no content present in v6. If we want to stick to the paradigm of being able to use `git/git-annex` with datalad datasets (which I think we should!), we need to be able to deal with that anyway. Datalad logic, that can't handle it, is invalid for v6. Simple as that in my opinion.\nSecond, it's needed for file system with no symlinks. So again - we need to be able to deal with that.\nThird, at some point v6 might become default mode of operation for `git-annex`, which leads to the same point: We would behave unpredictable for `git-annex` users at some point, if we were enforcing a different representation and we suddenly wouldn't be able to handle a perfectly fine annex repository.\nI simply cannot see how we wouldn't get into deep trouble, if we tried to somehow enforce symlinks.\n\nSo, to me it pretty much boils down to: Do we want to go for a default usage paradigm, where we lock (or unlock) files when committing by default pretty much similar to the always-clean-approach?\nWe would still need to be able to deal with mixed repos (at the very very least be able to detect it - instead of mistake a pointer file's content for the actual content - and fail with \"please lock/unlock everything\") and the always-lock default behavior would need to come with a `--dont-lock` switch, analogous to `--no-save` (or in case of an always-unlock-approach, it would be `--lock`).\n\n\u003e I am asking myself:\n\u003e - are there legitimate reasons to mix locked and unlocked files on symlink-capabale FSs? I think so, for the reasons of additional disk space demand alone (ignoring `annex.thin` and its implications for now).\n\nI agree. Also committing an unlocked (and locked for other files) state might help with `run`, particularly if a complex thing to be run is to be called by a user who can't easily tell what are the output files that need to be unlocked and can't easily automatically be detected either. If he can be pointed to a readily mixed state to base the call on, that might help.\n\n\u003e - do usability issue from dereferencing symlinks outweigh usability issue from not easily seeing absent content from missing symlink targets? Here I am leaning towards preferring to see the former to be no longer an issue, as it affects inspecting the \"final\" state of a dataset, where the latter is mostly a concern of (inappropriately) working with a dataset.\n\nI don't think I entirely got your thoughts here, @mih . In what sense is the state concerned with the first issue \"final\" and how does this make the issue disappear?\nRegarding the second issue: All depends on what \"easily seeing\" means. Since I think we need to be able to detect those files anyway, we can make them visible via `datalad ls` or something similiar.\n\n\n\u003e There are two advantages one has with symlinks:\n\u003e - Immediate failure on open() for any program, even those not aware of git-annex.\n\u003e - Protection against accidental overwriting of data.\n\u003e I'd argue that 2) is a non-issue, if the default usage paradigm of datalad is \"always-clean\". And I'd argue that the impact of 1) is massively different depending on whether we are looking at the data consumer case (I obtained a dataset from elsewhere) vs. the data producer case (I work with my own/already available data locally). In the latter case, data files without content are an exception (hence (1) is a non-issue), whereas in the former case they are the rule (and (1) is an issue.\n\nI'd argue that (2) doesn't necessarily become a non-issue just because you can clean it up. You might really want to fail early instead of discovering you changed files you shouldn't have changed days after a huge data processing pipeline was started. This may remain at big annoyance even if you started clean.\nI agree with what you said about (1) though.\n\nConclusion for me: Make always-lock the default behavior (which can be turned off per call as well as entirely via config), but way more importantly be able to accept mixed repositories and not mess them up.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1537788753,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzk0NTA1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2862#issuecomment-423945053"},"message":"Just to be clear: I do think, that always-unlock would be more intuitive for a lot of potential users. But I'd rather like them to know how to avoid issues by unlocking files, than require them to know how to clean up if things went wrong.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1537795250,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzk3MTg4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2862#issuecomment-423971885"},"message":"yet to digest in full but wanted to complement the list of \"There are two advantages one has with symlinks:\" with points which were possibly mentioned  separately and not explicitly linked to \"advantages\"\n\n3) efficient storage usage - no data duplication (unless in unlocked state, probably partially mitigated on CoW file systems in other modes)\n4) super fast branches manipulation - checking out another branch with a completely different set of files (annex view, git-annex branch, etc) is very fast regardless either you have content for those files or not.\n\nI do rely on those two features quite frequently.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1537795442,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzk3MjkxNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2862#issuecomment-423972916"},"message":"re  \"do usability issue from dereferencing symlinks outweigh usability issue from not easily seeing absent content from missing symlink targets? \"   I wondered if it could be partially (or fully?) mitigated by making those files without content not even readable  (by the git-annex itself)\n```shell\nhopa:/tmp\n$\u003e chmod a-rwx log.txt\n\n$\u003e cat log.txt\ncat: log.txt: Permission denied\n```\nit would blow upon read access, but still get information about size etc for such commands as du etc.  Didn't try if possible yet on crippled FSs, so may be it is not a solution","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1537803301,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNDAxOTE3Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2862#issuecomment-424019172"},"message":"@bpoldrack:\n\n\u003e Conclusion for me: Make always-lock the default behavior (which can be turned off per call as well as entirely via config), [\u0026#x2026;]\n\nI lean towards that default too, but regardless of the default I agree that being able to control it globally and per-call (e.g., `--lock=(default|yes|no)`) is important. I think which approach makes sense\u0026#x2014;always/mostly locked or always/mostly unlocked\u0026#x2014;will vary a lot between users or even between a user's different projects (e.g., one project frequently uses software that doesn't play well with symlinks).","files":null}]}